/**
@page cosmology_references References and implementation provenance

@section references_physics Scientific references

- Background growing-mode integral: Heath @cite heath1977.
- Gaussian CDM transfer shape: BBKS @cite bbks1986. The q convention and
  finite sigma8 cutoff in this implementation are explicitly documented in
  @ref cosmology_model; citation alone does not define those implementation choices.
- First-order Lagrangian displacement: Zel'dovich @cite zeldovich1970.
- Fourier assignment/interlacing: Sefusatti et al. @cite sefusatti2016. The
  published accuracy results are not automatic guarantees for this code's grids.
- Native FastPM context: Feng et al. @cite feng2016fastpm. This harness selects
  ordinary plain PM; it does not exercise the paper's modified large-step method.
- COLA context only: Tassev et al. @cite tassev2013cola. COLA is not implemented here.
- GADGET-2 TreePM: Springel @cite springel2005gadget2. The comparison uses
  GADGET-2.0.7, periodic TreePM and its declared parameter file, not GADGET-4.
- IPPL/PIC infrastructure: Muralikrishnan et al. @cite muralikrishnan2024scaling.
- Execution portability and distributed FFT infrastructure:
  Kokkos @cite trott2022kokkos and heFFTe @cite ayala2020heffte.

@section references_native_rng Native Fourier-mode random draws

CosmologyICRandom.h documents the exact `mode_hash_v1` byte layout and its
mapping to signed physical modes. Three references serve different purposes:

- NIST FIPS 180-4 @cite nist2015sha256 specifies SHA-256: padding and parsing
  (Secs. 5.1.1/5.2.1), initial state (Sec. 5.3.3), functions and constants
  (Secs. 4.1.2/4.2.2), and compression (Sec. 6.2.2). Our fixed message, seed/mode
  serialization and digest-to-uniform conversion are application conventions.
- Box and Muller @cite box1958normal gives the real-normal transform. We divide
  its complex combination by @f$\sqrt{2}@f$ so ideal real/imaginary variances are
  each 1/2, giving @f$E[|G|^2]=1@f$. Finite midpoint uniforms approximate that
  ideal model; they do not produce a mathematically continuous distribution.
- Salmon et al. @cite salmon2011parallel explains stateless keyed generation in
  parallel simulations. The authors maintain the
  <a href="https://github.com/DEShawResearch/random123">Random123 implementation</a>.
  It is conceptual context: this code uses SHA-256 for compatibility with the
  saved Python fixture and does not implement Philox, Threefry or Random123.

The published Random123 statistical/performance results do not qualify this
custom mapping. Its evidence consists of independent fixed-vector checks and
native-versus-saved IC validation. The Python `gaussian_fixture.py` protocol and
`TestCosmologyICRandom.cpp` vectors define compatibility; a protocol-changing
optimization needs a new RNG version and new evidence.

@section references_sources Native sources and compatibility boundaries

FastPM is pinned by the reference builder to commit
`15b6c4fd7502a81d99dd13f54fcc9cfa44be1331` of
<a href="https://github.com/fastpm/fastpm">the upstream FastPM repository</a>.
The harness adapts initialization, centering, convention conversion and CSV
output; it links native operators and ordinary native evolution without
reimplementing force or kick/drift kernels. Its source/build manifests record
what was actually compiled. Float32 particle fields and native Nyquist behavior
are retained, not hidden by broad equivalence claims.

The GADGET source archive and documentation are available from the
<a href="https://wwwmpa.mpa-garching.mpg.de/gadget/">official GADGET site</a>.
MPI FFTW version 2.x is required by GADGET-2; FFTW3 is not a drop-in replacement.
The portable local builder checks archive SHA256 values and uses command-line
Makefile options rather than modifying upstream simulation sources. Machine
configuration, compiler identity and PMGRID remain part of build provenance.

The Zarija initializer is a user-supplied local reference, not a published
HACC evolution oracle. The separate public-API probe is compiled against its
unmodified source; its ABI/precision and transfer-table first-row normalization
require explicit checking. The local manuals/source files, executable hashes
and reference manifests are implementation provenance rather than an invented
journal citation. No whole-code feature parity is claimed.

@section references_docs Documentation tooling

Formula syntax, bibliography support and warning settings follow the
<a href="https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/">official Doxygen manual</a>.
@ref cosmology_documentation_quality records what the documentation checks cover.
References provide rationale and context; implemented formulas and actual
qualification must be verified against source and retained campaign evidence.
*/
